1
00:05:24,942 --> 00:05:26,512
Good afternoon, everybody.

2
00:05:26,752 --> 00:05:27,952
Welcome back.

3
00:05:28,912 --> 00:05:33,952
Thank you once again for coming, and we are so, so thrilled that all of you are here today.

4
00:05:34,286 --> 00:05:42,788
As I mentioned this morning, we are here to mark the 70th anniversary of 1956 Dartmouth Workshop on AI.

5
00:05:43,726 --> 00:06:07,076
In recognition of that milestone, Dartmouth has created a new award, the AI at 70 Award, to recognize an individual who has moved this field forward, pushed us in new directions, and done so much without ever losing sight of the question that we're asking today, namely, what must remain human?

6
00:06:08,334 --> 00:06:13,764
I cannot imagine a person more fitting to receive this award.

7
00:06:14,574 --> 00:06:15,524
Jan Lukun.

8
00:06:16,110 --> 00:06:18,884
Jan is one of the pioneers of the modern AI.

9
00:06:19,182 --> 00:06:22,660
I got studied into this field because I read his paper.

10
00:06:23,310 --> 00:06:38,980
In the 1980s and 1990s, his work on neural networks has helped to teach computers to recognize handwritten digits and images, laying the foundation for many of today's technologies that we use every day.

11
00:06:39,790 --> 00:06:42,832
He spent the decades since advancing the field.

12
00:06:43,118 --> 00:06:46,992
At Bell Labs, he pioneered researching computer vision.

13
00:06:47,342 --> 00:06:51,140
At NYU, he helped establish the Center for Data Science.

14
00:06:51,694 --> 00:06:59,460
And at Meta, he founded his AI Research Lab and he served as the chief AI scientist for 12 years.

15
00:07:00,046 --> 00:07:13,476
In 2018, Jan received the Turing Award, often called the Nobel Prize in Computing, for breakthroughs that made deep learning a cornerstone of modern-day computing.

16
00:07:14,414 --> 00:07:19,712
And today, as a professor at NYU, he just told me that he still teaches, which is great.

17
00:07:20,352 --> 00:07:30,052
And executive chairman of AMI Labs, he's pursuing a different approach to AI, one that moves beyond today's limitations.

18
00:07:30,752 --> 00:07:42,052
To develop machines that can understand, they can think, they can predict, they can interact while keeping human at the center of this advancement.

19
00:07:42,672 --> 00:07:49,412
Seven years ago, the researchers here who gathered were asking questions that no one else was asking.

20
00:07:50,286 --> 00:07:51,872
Jan is the same way.

21
00:07:51,982 --> 00:07:57,284
He has dedicated his own career to pursuing knowledge in that same spirit.

22
00:07:58,286 --> 00:08:01,312
So without any further ado, I want to introduce Jan.

23
00:08:01,552 --> 00:08:02,832
Jan, if you could come.

24
00:08:03,150 --> 00:08:04,832
And on behalf of Dartmouth.

25
00:08:10,832 --> 00:08:16,772
On behalf of Dartmouth, we are so thrilled to present this AI Seminary World.

26
00:08:19,872 --> 00:08:22,672
And um thank you one.

27
00:08:22,992 --> 00:08:26,660
Once again, we are honored to have you here in Hanover.

28
00:08:27,022 --> 00:08:35,268
And now I'm pleased to hand it over to you for your keynote world models enabling the next AI revolution.

29
00:08:35,534 --> 00:08:37,712
Please welcome him once again.

30
00:08:45,486 --> 00:08:46,128
Thank you so much.

31
00:08:46,478 --> 00:08:49,028
It's a real pleasure to be here.

32
00:08:49,614 --> 00:08:54,532
I think my last time, my last talk at Dartmouth was eight years ago.

33
00:08:55,054 --> 00:08:57,348
I was trying to remember exactly when that was.

34
00:08:59,566 --> 00:09:01,860
we'll see if I come again eight years from now.

35
00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:15,488
So I'll talk about the future of AI largely, also a bit about the past or the current state of affairs.

36
00:09:16,302 --> 00:09:25,092
and what I think is that true progress in AI will come because of new concepts.

37
00:09:25,678 --> 00:09:27,428
We're not done yet.

38
00:09:28,110 --> 00:09:39,328
Despite what you might hear from some of the more optimistic CEOs of Silicon Valley, we're not anywhere close to human-level AI, which some people call AGI.

39
00:09:39,488 --> 00:09:40,356
I hate that phrase.

40
00:09:41,008 --> 00:09:42,500
I'll tell you why in a minute.

41
00:09:44,686 --> 00:09:47,248
and there is this question of what must remain human.

42
00:09:47,408 --> 00:09:48,900
I'm not going to answer that question.

43
00:09:50,286 --> 00:09:53,088
unless you ask me afterwards.

44
00:09:53,540 --> 00:09:56,420
Okay, so first of all, AI sucks.

45
00:09:58,208 --> 00:10:09,888
We certainly made a lot of progress in the last 70 years towards making machines more intelligent and more useful, generally, but we have AI systems that can write code.

46
00:10:10,048 --> 00:10:13,408
they can pass the bar exam, they can win math, olympia.

47
00:10:13,488 --> 00:10:18,660
they can prove theorems that's, you know, stayed and proven for decades.

48
00:10:19,728 --> 00:10:22,608
but where is our domestic robot?

49
00:10:22,688 --> 00:10:25,488
Where is our level five cell-driving cars?

50
00:10:25,968 --> 00:10:26,788
we don't have those.

51
00:10:27,470 --> 00:10:40,324
And so that indicates that AI, while AI can deal with discrete symbols, language, code, mathematics, it still has a very hard time dealing with the real world.

52
00:10:40,718 --> 00:10:42,660
The real world is complicated, it's messy.

53
00:10:43,648 --> 00:10:47,876
And current AI approaches basically are kind of helpless for it.

54
00:10:50,062 --> 00:10:56,528
so AI really can't deal with high-dimensional continuous data like images, video, audio, etc.

55
00:10:56,768 --> 00:11:03,568
It can, of course, a little bit, but it's not the same technique that are used in AI assistant.

56
00:11:03,808 --> 00:11:16,368
Basically, what a lot of AI companies are doing at the moment is that they are kind of bolting all kinds of modules to allow AI systems to solve problems using tools to perceive the world with vision, using vision.

57
00:11:16,368 --> 00:11:20,356
encoders and various other things, understand speech.

58
00:11:20,448 --> 00:11:27,968
but those are, you know, extra modules that are trained separately and bolted onto LLMs, LLM, still being the central part.

59
00:11:28,048 --> 00:11:28,772
the centerpiece.

60
00:11:29,728 --> 00:11:42,276
We don't really have general learning techniques that allow an AI system to understand high-dimensional continuous noisy signals like video, audio, sensor data, etc.

61
00:11:43,408 --> 00:11:56,608
We do have a genetic systems, but they need to be trained by imitation, basically reproducing human behavior, and require a lot of examples or massive simulation environments.

62
00:11:59,530 --> 00:12:05,728
and they don't have the ability to anticipate the consequences of their actions, which is really a very bad way to act.

63
00:12:06,062 --> 00:12:14,404
If you can't anticipate what's going to happen as a consequence of actions you're taking, you might be doing something really bad without realizing.

64
00:12:16,128 --> 00:12:18,928
so there's a number of questions.

65
00:12:19,088 --> 00:12:26,116
Why can't AI systems solve new problems zero shot without being trained to solve them with lots of examples?

66
00:12:26,574 --> 00:12:27,812
Why can't they have common sense?

67
00:12:29,248 --> 00:12:32,288
and the answer is the real world is messy, as I was saying.

68
00:12:32,462 --> 00:12:34,276
Language is simple, surprisingly.

69
00:12:36,688 --> 00:12:39,648
never mind trying to reach human-level intelligence with AI.

70
00:12:39,728 --> 00:12:46,688
We can't even reach the level of understanding the physical world that a cat or a dog can reach with robots.

71
00:12:47,888 --> 00:12:50,244
so clearly we're missing something quite big.

72
00:12:52,608 --> 00:12:58,468
we don't have AI systems that can do what a 10 year old can do the first time you ask them, like clearing up the dinner table, for example.

73
00:13:01,422 --> 00:13:05,860
We have billions of hours of training data of cars being driven by people.

74
00:13:06,190 --> 00:13:10,436
We should be able to train a neural net to just emulate human behavior for driving.

75
00:13:11,310 --> 00:13:12,368
That has been a complete failure.

76
00:13:12,448 --> 00:13:13,808
This is called mutation learning.

77
00:13:13,888 --> 00:13:15,648
It's been essentially a failure.

78
00:13:17,248 --> 00:13:22,852
The most autonomous cars that we have are level four, they're not level five because they can only drive in certain areas.

79
00:13:23,758 --> 00:13:33,924
And they use all kinds of heavily engineered systems to be able to do that and detailed maps and lidar and all kinds of stuff.

80
00:13:34,288 --> 00:13:47,300
So we keep bumping into this paradox called the Moravec paradox, which was formulated by Hans Moravec, a roboticist in 1988, that things that are easy for humans are difficult for AI, and vice versa.

81
00:13:48,686 --> 00:13:51,108
And when you say humans, it's also animals.

82
00:13:51,488 --> 00:13:55,488
So perhaps that begs the question of what really is intelligence, right?

83
00:13:56,048 --> 00:14:03,524
we have this impression that the AI assistants that we use, all of us use every day, are intelligent.

84
00:14:03,918 --> 00:14:08,516
They're not that intelligent, except perhaps for code and mathematics.

85
00:14:08,814 --> 00:14:11,408
But for the rest, they're not that intelligent.

86
00:14:11,568 --> 00:14:15,488
They're very good at retrieving known knowledge.

87
00:14:15,758 --> 00:14:21,924
They can accumulate enormous amounts of declarative knowledge and regurgitate it at the right time.

88
00:14:22,528 --> 00:14:24,128
But that's not intelligence.

89
00:14:24,368 --> 00:14:26,528
So this is a quote, which is actually apocryphal.

90
00:14:26,768 --> 00:14:29,888
Jean Piaget never actually said this explicitly, but that's what he meant.

91
00:14:30,094 --> 00:14:35,008
And a lot of people who wrote about his work kind of formulated it this way.

92
00:14:35,470 --> 00:14:42,928
Jean-Piaget was a developmental psychologist that studied how children basically become intelligent.

93
00:14:43,374 --> 00:14:47,780
So he said, intelligence is not what you know, it's what you do when you don't know.

94
00:14:48,430 --> 00:14:52,768
And so he loved that quote because that really says that you're facing a new situation.

95
00:14:54,190 --> 00:14:54,916
What are you going to do?

96
00:14:55,342 --> 00:14:58,788
You've never been trained to handle that before.

97
00:14:59,374 --> 00:15:04,068
You have to use the full power of your intelligence to figure out what to do.

98
00:15:05,008 --> 00:15:08,036
And regurgitating known knowledge is not intelligence.

99
00:15:08,590 --> 00:15:18,660
So the AI systems we have at the moment are of the type that regurgitate knowledge, but they don't really invent new knowledge, except in very narrow domains like code and mathematics.

100
00:15:21,134 --> 00:15:41,860
So we should measure intelligence not by a collection of skills or by whether a system can answer questions properly, but by how fast a system can learn a new task or a new skill, possibly zero shot without requiring any prior training.

101
00:15:44,494 --> 00:15:51,460
The other question that we need to figure out is how do humans and animals learn?

102
00:15:52,366 --> 00:15:59,108
So this chart indicates at what age in months babies learn basic concepts about the world, really very basic concepts.

103
00:16:00,208 --> 00:16:08,580
this was put together by my former colleague from Meta Emmanuel Dubout, who is a cognitive scientist.

104
00:16:09,902 --> 00:16:12,260
And there are things that appear pretty early in life.

105
00:16:13,008 --> 00:16:18,948
the ability to distinguish between animate and inanimate objects, that's around two months.

106
00:16:19,886 --> 00:16:24,580
Object permanence around two months, the notion of natural categories.

107
00:16:25,166 --> 00:16:29,284
Babies know the difference between the table and the chair, even though they don't know the names.

108
00:16:30,530 --> 00:16:38,532
But things like gravity inertia, conservation of momentum, what we could call intuitive physics, that only pops up around nine months.

109
00:16:40,848 --> 00:16:42,528
And so it takes a while.

110
00:16:42,608 --> 00:16:44,848
It takes a lot of observation of the world.

111
00:16:44,928 --> 00:16:46,180
It also takes interaction.

112
00:16:46,688 --> 00:16:53,088
But a lot of it is learned mostly by observation, particularly in the first three or four months, where babies basically cannot do anything in the world.

113
00:16:53,168 --> 00:16:55,396
They can gesticulate, but that's about it.

114
00:16:57,070 --> 00:17:07,300
So perhaps the answer of what is intelligence is the ability to handle new situations that have never the system has never faced before.

115
00:17:09,678 --> 00:17:13,808
Co-wrote a little piece with some of my younger colleagues.

116
00:17:13,888 --> 00:17:15,140
They did all the work, really.

117
00:17:15,488 --> 00:17:20,288
Judah is a postdoc in my lab now, Ravid was a postdoc before.

118
00:17:21,902 --> 00:17:24,484
Adaptation really is kind of a key.

119
00:17:24,974 --> 00:17:26,528
Okay, here is another element.

120
00:17:28,078 --> 00:17:32,932
LLMs are trained on human-produced text, of which there is a limited amount.

121
00:17:33,678 --> 00:17:41,860
All the publicly available text on the internet represents roughly two 10 to the 13 words, 20 trillion words.

122
00:17:43,168 --> 00:17:51,460
You turn those words into tokens, you get roughly 30 trillion, 30 um trillion words.

123
00:17:52,142 --> 00:17:57,008
Each token is three bytes, and so you do the arithmetics and you get roughly 10 to the 14 bytes.

124
00:17:57,088 --> 00:18:02,208
That's the size of the entire corpus that LLMs are pre-trained on, right?

125
00:18:02,478 --> 00:18:05,860
They're fine-tuned with way more data, but that's kind of the pre-training phase.

126
00:18:06,414 --> 00:18:11,716
Now compare this with a uh, it would take any of us, by the way, about half a million years or so to read through this.

127
00:18:12,462 --> 00:18:21,888
Take a human child, a four-year-old, has been awake a total of about 16,000 hours, which by the way is a very small amount of video, it's about 30 minutes of YouTube uploads.

128
00:18:22,478 --> 00:18:31,716
we have two million optic nerve fibers carrying visual information to the brain, each carrying about one byte per second, roughly.

129
00:18:32,398 --> 00:18:39,108
So the data volume that a four-year-old has seen through vision, also through touch, is about 10 to the 14 bytes.

130
00:18:39,822 --> 00:18:43,972
And that tells you that we're not going to get to human-level AI by just training on text.

131
00:18:44,910 --> 00:18:50,368
LLMs, in and of themselves, are never going to reach human-level intelligence.

132
00:18:54,414 --> 00:19:06,308
okay, then here is another question: what type of inference are we using when we think?

133
00:19:10,446 --> 00:19:16,804
are we producing all the things that we're thinking of by forward propagation through a feed-forward neural net?

134
00:19:18,926 --> 00:19:25,572
The answer is obviously no, because we we tend to think longer on problems that are more complex, right?

135
00:19:26,254 --> 00:19:32,868
So obviously we have a reasoning process is something that can take a variable amount of time depending on the complexity of the task.

136
00:19:33,168 --> 00:19:33,408
Right?

137
00:19:33,488 --> 00:19:35,780
We think about things before we act most of the time.

138
00:19:36,750 --> 00:19:38,916
Except for tasks that we are really familiar with.

139
00:19:39,278 --> 00:19:41,008
Okay, this is not the way LLMs work.

140
00:19:41,168 --> 00:19:49,668
LLMs take a sequence of words, propagate it through a bunch of layers of a transformer, and then produce one token.

141
00:19:49,902 --> 00:19:51,364
It's purely forward propagation.

142
00:19:53,070 --> 00:20:03,588
so if you ask an LLM to answer a question by yes or no, the amount of computation that will be devoted to this answer does not depend on the complexity of the question.

143
00:20:04,142 --> 00:20:08,208
So the trick that people play with LLM is that it's chain of thought.

144
00:20:08,608 --> 00:20:17,284
you get the LLM to produce lots and lots and lots of tokens, basically explaining its process of thinking.

145
00:20:17,728 --> 00:20:23,812
and in fact, you can explicitly train it to go through particular steps of reasoning, and that's called chain of thoughts.

146
00:20:25,408 --> 00:20:30,528
But it's really not a good principle by which a system could reason.

147
00:20:30,768 --> 00:20:46,532
Classically in AI, if we go back to good old-fashioned AI or even in Bayesian models and things like that, inference is not a process of forward propagation to a bunch of layers in a computational system.

148
00:20:47,008 --> 00:20:49,892
It's generally a search or an optimization.

149
00:20:51,246 --> 00:21:01,728
So this is the diagram that you see on the right, where you perceive the world, you get some idea of the state of the world, and then you imagine an action.

150
00:21:01,966 --> 00:21:12,516
Let's say, if it's an agent or a robot, you imagine an action, and then you check whether this action actually accomplishes the task you want to accomplish through an objective function.

151
00:21:13,408 --> 00:21:27,248
think of this square or rectangular box, the red box, as a scalar-valued function that takes an input and a proposed output and tells you if this output is a good output for the input.

152
00:21:27,438 --> 00:21:32,228
Okay, it measures the degree of compatibility between a proposed output and an input.

153
00:21:33,934 --> 00:21:47,908
And so the inference process there is the system searches for a proposed action that is optimal, that will minimize this objective function, and then it outputs this action to the world.

154
00:21:50,128 --> 00:21:57,284
So inference by optimization is computationally intrinsically more powerful than through forward propagation.

155
00:21:57,742 --> 00:22:09,648
Okay, I have a big caveat to say here, because in principle, you could actually reduce any computation to a forward propagation to a two-layer network.

156
00:22:09,728 --> 00:22:21,648
There's a very famous theorem by someone who is in the audience, George Ivanko, who uh from 1989 that showed that neural nets, uh two-layer neural nets are universal approximators.

157
00:22:21,728 --> 00:22:23,328
So in principle, you don't need anything else.

158
00:22:23,488 --> 00:22:25,008
But there's a question of efficiency, right?

159
00:22:25,248 --> 00:22:34,608
The reason why we train neural nets with multiple layers is because they have practical size, whether if we only want to make them two layers, the size is impractical.

160
00:22:34,894 --> 00:22:42,980
So there's kind of a similar type of more careful argument here, which I'm not going to go into belabor on.

161
00:22:43,488 --> 00:22:45,728
but essentially we have those two models, right?

162
00:22:45,888 --> 00:22:51,396
Uh uh inferenced by forward propagation to a neural net, and then inferenced by optimization.

163
00:22:52,238 --> 00:23:07,328
And inference by optimization would require some way of evaluating what the consequences of a particular action or sequence of actions will be, and then feeding this to an objective that measures to what extent the task has been accomplished.

164
00:23:07,566 --> 00:23:14,688
Now, those two types of inference actually exist in humans and animals, and psychologists call them system one and system two.

165
00:23:14,848 --> 00:23:21,968
System one is tasks that you accomplish without really thinking consciously about them, because you're so used to doing them.

166
00:23:22,158 --> 00:23:29,968
And system two is when you use the full power of your mind to accomplish a task.

167
00:23:31,248 --> 00:23:35,008
so basically we should be working on system two.

168
00:23:35,168 --> 00:23:38,724
How do we get AI systems to do system two?

169
00:23:39,408 --> 00:23:50,848
So I sort of this is a collection of ideas I've been kind of thinking about and working on for several decades, but they sort of gelled around four years ago, 2022.

170
00:23:50,928 --> 00:23:52,848
So I wrote this paper that I posted online.

171
00:23:53,168 --> 00:23:59,328
It passed towards autonomous machine intelligence that sort of explains my vision for where AI should go over the next 10 years.

172
00:23:59,408 --> 00:24:03,748
I don't know about 20 years, but the next 10 years.

173
00:24:04,208 --> 00:24:13,956
20 years because there was a series of conferences here at Dartmouth 20 years ago with some of the people who were here in 1956.

174
00:24:15,438 --> 00:24:18,528
and so you know I don't know what's gonna happen over the next 20 years.

175
00:24:18,688 --> 00:24:26,308
Perhaps we'll have AI system that reach human intelligence, but like I wouldn't bet on it because it's almost certainly more complicated than we think.

176
00:24:27,918 --> 00:24:33,284
but if we have a chance of reaching that, I think it would have to be based on world models and inference by optimization.

177
00:24:35,022 --> 00:24:37,248
so here's the link to the paper.

178
00:24:37,328 --> 00:24:40,644
There is other versions of this talk that you can watch possibly.

179
00:24:41,008 --> 00:24:50,288
Okay, so this sort of new type of architecture I call objective-driven uh AI, uh, objective-driven architecture, really.

180
00:24:50,606 --> 00:24:51,876
Okay, perceive the world.

181
00:24:52,302 --> 00:24:55,876
It's called the observation on the world, it's called X at time t, XT.

182
00:24:56,334 --> 00:24:59,328
Run through some perception encoder that you're gonna have to train.

183
00:24:59,408 --> 00:25:03,088
It gives you an idea of the current state of the world, at least the part that you can observe.

184
00:25:03,374 --> 00:25:16,644
Run this through a world model, which is a trainable function, some sort of neural net, that given the current state of the world and given a proposed action, A of T, produces a prediction for the next state of the world, SP of T plus 1.

185
00:25:17,390 --> 00:25:19,396
Okay, state prediction.

186
00:25:20,462 --> 00:25:23,168
You feed that to an objective function.

187
00:25:23,328 --> 00:25:27,728
The objective function tells you the task you set out to accomplish has been accomplished or not.

188
00:25:27,982 --> 00:25:34,820
It gives you zero if the task has been accomplished and some positive number function of how far from accomplishing the task you are.

189
00:25:35,408 --> 00:25:43,716
And the system works by basically searching for an action or a sequence of actions that minimizes this objective.

190
00:25:47,328 --> 00:25:56,292
Now, of course, if you have a world model that can produce the next state of the world, you can apply it multiple times so that you can predict the consequence of a sequence of actions instead of a single one.

191
00:25:57,088 --> 00:25:58,848
But conceptually it's the same thing.

192
00:25:59,054 --> 00:26:03,620
I added another component here called the guardrail objectives.

193
00:26:04,014 --> 00:26:12,196
You'd like to be able to design the system in such a way that nobody gets hurt when the system accomplishes a sequence of actions.

194
00:26:34,288 --> 00:26:50,020
But at least you have a way of making those systems intrinsically safe by training guardrail objectives that if they are optimized or satisfied by the optimization process that figures out the sequence of actions, the system will not hurt anyone.

195
00:26:50,318 --> 00:26:50,608
Okay?

196
00:26:50,848 --> 00:27:04,388
So it could be very simple, low-level guardrails, like you have this robot cook in your kitchen, and you can have a very low-level rule that says, well, if there are people around and you have a big knife in your hand, don't flail your arms, right?

197
00:27:05,070 --> 00:27:11,748
Or you can have more higher level concepts depending on the type of representation, of the state of the world that you have.

198
00:27:13,102 --> 00:27:22,048
So this process of optimizing a sequence of actions according to a model of the system you're controlling to minimize the cost is very classical in optimal control.

199
00:27:22,128 --> 00:27:31,008
It's called model predictive control with a few more ingredients if you do it with receding horizon and everything.

200
00:27:31,168 --> 00:27:32,688
But it's not a new concept.

201
00:27:32,768 --> 00:27:37,488
The new concept is we're going to train the world model with neural nets.

202
00:27:37,568 --> 00:27:40,288
And there is another new concept which is hierarchical planning.

203
00:27:40,448 --> 00:27:47,748
So this is a problem that until last year or perhaps two years ago would have said is completely unsolved.

204
00:27:48,288 --> 00:27:55,008
but at the end of this talk, I'm going to show you some evidence that we are on the way to making progress there.

205
00:27:55,248 --> 00:27:56,964
So, what is hierarchical planning?

206
00:27:58,574 --> 00:28:01,248
your world model is not a single model in your brain.

207
00:28:01,488 --> 00:28:13,604
you need multiple models for different situations, and you need models to be able to predict things that will happen in the next 200 milliseconds, or things that will happen in the next second, in the next minute, in the next hour, maybe the next year.

208
00:28:14,094 --> 00:28:21,408
Okay, so if I take an object and I tell you I'm going to open my hand, you know this object is gonna fall due to gravity, right?

209
00:28:21,678 --> 00:28:24,928
You don't need you know superpowers to do this.

210
00:28:25,198 --> 00:28:26,608
and it's relatively short term.

211
00:28:26,768 --> 00:28:35,556
You can't exactly predict exactly where the thing is gonna fall or in which configuration, but you can make some level of prediction, right?

212
00:28:37,550 --> 00:28:45,220
you can predict if you send some muscle control to your arm how your arm is going to be 100 milliseconds from now.

213
00:28:46,414 --> 00:28:53,316
If you want to plan a trip, let's say I'm sitting in my NYU office in New York and I want to be in Paris tomorrow.

214
00:28:54,800 --> 00:29:03,204
I cannot possibly plan my entire trip in terms of muscle control, which is the low-level actions that I can take with my body.

215
00:29:04,718 --> 00:29:08,612
I have to make this planning at a much higher level of abstraction.

216
00:29:09,326 --> 00:29:16,036
I need to be able to, for example, realize that if I want to be in Paris tomorrow, I need to go to the airport and catch a plane.

217
00:29:16,240 --> 00:29:18,080
That's a two-step plan.

218
00:29:18,702 --> 00:29:19,520
It's very high level.

219
00:29:19,600 --> 00:29:24,164
I don't need to know much about the state of the world to be able to tell that I need to get to the airport and catch a plane.

220
00:29:24,494 --> 00:29:26,564
Now I have a sub-goal, getting to the airport.

221
00:29:27,054 --> 00:29:31,300
I'm in New York, so I need to go on the street and hail a taxi for the airport.

222
00:29:31,758 --> 00:29:33,920
Now I have a sub-sub goal, beyond the street.

223
00:29:34,254 --> 00:29:37,284
I need to go to the elevator, push the button, walk out the building.

224
00:29:37,678 --> 00:29:40,640
Now I have a sub-sub sub-goal, getting to the elevator.

225
00:29:41,518 --> 00:29:47,840
I need to stand up from my chair, pick up my bag, open my door, shut my door, walk, avoiding obstacles.

226
00:29:48,142 --> 00:29:54,468
And again, I cannot plan this in terms of muscle control because there might be obstacles on my route that I haven't foreseen.

227
00:29:55,280 --> 00:30:07,200
So, you know, you have to sort of go down in this hierarchy down to a point where an action consists in, for example, standing up from a chair, you don't need to plan this, right?

228
00:30:07,280 --> 00:30:12,036
You can just apply a uh, it's a task that you're so used to doing, you don't have to plan.

229
00:30:14,030 --> 00:30:21,476
but this question of solving the hierarchical planning problem is absolutely fundamental if you want to build really intelligent systems.

230
00:30:21,902 --> 00:30:29,796
What distinguishes human intelligence from a lot of animal intelligence is this hierarchy or the number of levels in this hierarchy.

231
00:30:32,526 --> 00:30:34,372
so we're going to have to train hierarchical world models.

232
00:30:35,360 --> 00:30:37,920
and I'll tell you in a minute how this is done.

233
00:30:38,190 --> 00:30:42,020
Okay, now the next question is how are we going to train those world models if we need world models?

234
00:30:43,120 --> 00:30:49,156
so one option is to use generative model, a generative architecture.

235
00:30:49,614 --> 00:30:52,960
Generative AI is what we call AI now, right?

236
00:30:53,120 --> 00:30:54,852
All of AI is generative AI.

237
00:30:55,760 --> 00:31:00,612
and I'm going to tell you if you want to train world models, they should not be generative.

238
00:31:01,134 --> 00:31:01,360
Okay?

239
00:31:02,580 --> 00:31:07,680
It Doesn't make me very popular, but okay, so generative, right?

240
00:31:08,238 --> 00:31:13,508
LLMs are trained to predict the next word in a sequence of words, essentially.

241
00:31:14,382 --> 00:31:21,060
Why don't we use the same technique to train a system to understand the real world, train it to predict what's going to happen in a video, for example?

242
00:31:21,678 --> 00:31:38,820
I've been working on this for the better part of the last 20 years, way before LLMs, because I was always convinced that this training system to predict what's going to happen in a video will drive the system to understand the underlying nature of the world and perhaps learn things like common sense and intuitive physics.

243
00:31:40,110 --> 00:31:48,868
I failed largely for the first 15 of those 20 years, and about five years ago realized the reason I failed is because I was trying to use generative models.

244
00:31:50,480 --> 00:31:54,564
I realized this before LLMs became fashionable.

245
00:31:55,918 --> 00:31:56,400
Why?

246
00:31:56,718 --> 00:32:04,644
The reason is most of the information in a video or in a natural signal, whatever it is, is completely unpredictable.

247
00:32:05,678 --> 00:32:11,236
And so when you train a system to predict all the details in that data, you kind of kill it.

248
00:32:12,160 --> 00:32:13,440
let me take an example.

249
00:32:13,600 --> 00:32:23,040
If I shoot a video of this room, I start from here, I rotate the camera slowly, I stop here, and I ask the system predict the rest of the video.

250
00:32:23,534 --> 00:32:26,084
It's gonna predict the camera, it's gonna continue rotating.

251
00:32:26,560 --> 00:32:31,076
there's going to be people sitting in seats, probably going to be a wall at some point.

252
00:32:33,806 --> 00:32:37,360
but there's absolutely no way it can predict what all of you look like, right?

253
00:32:37,550 --> 00:32:42,212
That information is just not present in the initial segment of the video.

254
00:32:42,894 --> 00:32:44,880
And that's most of the information in the video.

255
00:32:45,006 --> 00:32:46,372
That's completely unpredictable.

256
00:32:47,440 --> 00:32:52,640
so you can train a generative model, you know, one of those diffusion models or whatever, to actually make something up.

257
00:32:52,814 --> 00:32:59,588
It's going to produce a kind of nice looking meeting room with you know people looking like people, but they're not going to look like you.

258
00:33:01,262 --> 00:33:03,204
You know, they're going to be hallucinated people.

259
00:33:03,920 --> 00:33:10,404
so if you want the system to really understand the underlying nature of the world, you don't want to force it to predict things that it simply cannot predict.

260
00:33:13,968 --> 00:33:23,408
And again, you know, going back 10, 15 years, we had projects on trying to do video prediction, and we're getting those horrible blurry results.

261
00:33:25,454 --> 00:33:35,248
you can mitigate the issue with latent variable models like diffusion models, which is what all the video generation systems use, but those systems don't really understand the underlying nature of the world.

262
00:33:35,502 --> 00:33:37,648
So my solution is something called JEPA.

263
00:33:37,808 --> 00:33:42,608
That means joint embedding predictive architecture, and it's shown on the right.

264
00:33:42,958 --> 00:33:58,048
So on the left, you have a generative architecture, given observation X, running through an encoder, an action that you imagine, or intervention, run that through a predictor, and then the system predicts a value for y.

265
00:33:58,382 --> 00:34:03,556
And to train the system, you minimize some divergence measure between the observed y and the predicted y.

266
00:34:04,974 --> 00:34:11,684
Y can be the future of a video where X is the current observation of the video.

267
00:34:12,302 --> 00:34:13,408
but it could be anything else.

268
00:34:13,614 --> 00:34:17,284
Y could be two different views of the same scene or whatever.

269
00:34:19,088 --> 00:34:22,020
the Jepa is shown on the right.

270
00:34:22,510 --> 00:34:30,768
So here you run both X and Y through encoders, and you do that prediction, but you do it in representation space.

271
00:34:30,848 --> 00:34:36,688
You never train the system to attempt to reconstruct every detail of Y.

272
00:34:37,230 --> 00:34:46,404
You only train it to predict an abstract representation of Y, hoping that the encoder will eliminate all the parts of Y that are unpredictable.

273
00:34:46,688 --> 00:34:46,928
Okay?

274
00:34:47,008 --> 00:34:47,808
That's JIPA.

275
00:34:48,048 --> 00:34:48,580
Nothing more.

276
00:34:52,302 --> 00:34:54,276
so LLMs are of the first type, right?

277
00:34:55,808 --> 00:35:03,972
so there's sort of various ways you can training a generative model is very simple, it's basically like supervised learning.

278
00:35:04,448 --> 00:35:10,500
training one of those joint embedding architectures is a lot harder because of something called the collapse problem.

279
00:35:11,022 --> 00:35:11,488
What does that mean?

280
00:35:11,648 --> 00:35:16,100
Let's say we're in the scenario on the left, which is a simplified JPAD, doesn't have a predictor.

281
00:35:16,768 --> 00:35:27,568
we have two views of the same scene, or corrupted view of original picture, we run those two images through encoder and we tell the system those two views are really the same object.

282
00:35:27,648 --> 00:35:33,764
Like whatever representation you extract from the image, they should be equal because it's really the same thing you're looking at.

283
00:35:35,278 --> 00:35:47,204
so if you just use that as the last function, that system collapses because what it does is that it ignores the inputs and it produces constant representations that are always equal.

284
00:35:49,488 --> 00:35:52,608
The one on the right is the same.

285
00:35:52,688 --> 00:36:02,660
You take an image or a video, you corrupt it, or you shift it in time if you want, and then you run both through encoders, try to predict the representation of y from the representation of X.

286
00:36:04,206 --> 00:36:12,292
The system can choose to ignore the input, produce constant representations, and now your prediction error is zero all the time, and your system does not do anything useful for you.

287
00:36:13,774 --> 00:36:15,088
So, how do you prevent that collapse?

288
00:36:15,278 --> 00:36:30,148
So, first of all, I want to say this idea that an intelligent system should extract abstract representation of the inputs that ignores a lot of details about the input, I think is absolutely fundamental.

289
00:36:31,374 --> 00:36:33,636
Which is why I'm telling people to abandon generative models.

290
00:36:34,030 --> 00:36:35,968
And the reason is the following.

291
00:36:37,614 --> 00:36:46,368
I could in principle explain everything that takes place in this room in terms of, let's say, quantum field theory, right?

292
00:36:46,702 --> 00:36:57,924
Could get the wave function of this room, or perhaps a big chunk of the universe around us, and then run some gigantic quantum simulation, and I could predict if one minute from now George is going to scratch his nose.

293
00:36:58,448 --> 00:36:58,724
All right.

294
00:36:59,854 --> 00:37:04,736
Okay, That's a full self-fulfilling prophecy.

295
00:37:07,566 --> 00:37:09,284
Okay, but that would be kind of crazy, right?

296
00:37:11,616 --> 00:37:29,616
it would be like a huge amount of computation, and also it would be completely impractical because we require some gigantic computing infrastructure that is out of reach, and it would probably not even work because a lot of processes involve sort of quantum entanglement with the rest of the universe.

297
00:37:29,696 --> 00:37:35,364
So, there's a lot of things that are just not predictable in practice, So, what do we do?

298
00:37:36,336 --> 00:37:37,696
we invent abstractions.

299
00:37:37,776 --> 00:37:55,748
If I give you a box full of air and I tell you I'm going to heat up this box by 10 degrees Celsius, Kelvin, you can tell because of the law of ideal gas that the pressure inside that box is going to increase proportionally to the temperature in Kelvin.

300
00:37:56,640 --> 00:38:06,084
PV equals NRT for those of you who have taken a classical physics course recently.

301
00:38:07,886 --> 00:38:15,876
you can make that prediction, even though the underlying process is molecules of air bumping into each other and onto the walls of the box.

302
00:38:16,142 --> 00:38:32,228
But there's no way you can simulate this process at that level of granularity, But still at a very abstract level of representation, where you represent the state of the box, with basically three numbers, you can make predictions.

303
00:38:32,526 --> 00:38:44,292
Okay, so the idea that the representation of the state of the system needs to be much more compact than the full state, and ignore all the stuff you cannot predict is fundamental to science.

304
00:38:44,622 --> 00:38:48,816
So this is why we invent all those abstractions, right?

305
00:38:49,710 --> 00:38:59,716
From quantum field theory, we invent the concept of particles and then atoms and then molecules and in the living world, proteins, organelles, cells, organisms, individual societies, ecosystems.

306
00:39:00,430 --> 00:39:12,388
Each level in this hierarchy describes the world with fewer details than the level below, but allows us to make longer term prediction on bigger systems, right?

307
00:39:13,376 --> 00:39:17,856
and that's I think essential to understanding the physical world.

308
00:39:17,936 --> 00:39:23,204
We have to invent abstractions that simplify the world, if you want.

309
00:39:23,854 --> 00:39:34,096
And the fact that the universe we live in lends itself to this sounds like a conspiracy to me, or a mystery.

310
00:39:34,446 --> 00:39:40,868
I think it's the same mystery that Einstein was talking about when he said the most incomprehensible thing about the world is that the world is comprehensible.

311
00:39:41,696 --> 00:39:43,332
I love that quote too.

312
00:39:46,990 --> 00:39:59,456
so every level in this hierarchy ignores some details about the level below, but contains knowledge that is not really derivable from the level below.

313
00:39:59,616 --> 00:40:05,956
Chemists know things about chemistry that atomic physicists don't, right?

314
00:40:06,734 --> 00:40:12,644
And biochemists certainly know things that chemists don't, and biologists know things that biochemists don't, etc.

315
00:40:14,478 --> 00:40:22,564
Uh so models of the world are not going to be world simulators or digital twins, they're going to be sort of abstract versions of it.

316
00:40:22,976 --> 00:40:29,856
And we should make them action condition predictors in abstract representation space so that we can do planning with them.

317
00:40:30,062 --> 00:40:34,096
Okay, so if we have this type of world model, we can use them for all kinds of stuff.

318
00:40:34,176 --> 00:40:46,816
Let's say we have a very complex system whose dynamics is so complex that we can't reduce it to a small number of equations, classically, uh like say a whole turbojet engine or turbofan.

319
00:40:47,694 --> 00:40:59,044
If we have a phenomenological model of the system that tells us, given the state of that system at time t and an intervention, I imagine taking, give me the state at time t plus one.

320
00:40:59,790 --> 00:41:08,256
then we can ultimately control that turbojet to maximize reliability or all kinds of criteria that we are interested in.

321
00:41:08,622 --> 00:41:12,548
Power consumption, CO2 emission, whatever.

322
00:41:13,038 --> 00:41:17,828
We can apply this to things like chemical plants or power plants or to a patient.

323
00:41:18,446 --> 00:41:22,468
Let's say we want to have a predictive model of the state of a patient.

324
00:41:23,136 --> 00:41:28,740
a patient is certainly a complex system that we can't completely simulate.

325
00:41:29,198 --> 00:41:33,124
So we need to have some pharmacological model of it, which is what physicians do, really.

326
00:41:34,958 --> 00:41:36,224
Or a robot, even.

327
00:41:36,654 --> 00:41:39,904
It's easy enough to model a robot with a bunch of equations.

328
00:41:40,110 --> 00:41:43,504
It's very hard to model the interaction of a robot with the real world.

329
00:41:43,758 --> 00:41:55,424
Which is why you see those really impressive humanoid robots from a bunch of Chinese companies doing Kung Fu and you know running and everything.

330
00:41:55,504 --> 00:41:58,596
There's no interaction with the real world other than touching the ground.

331
00:41:59,086 --> 00:42:09,156
As soon as you want them to do something like manipulation or clearing up the dinner table, as I was saying earlier, they don't work.

332
00:42:10,446 --> 00:42:14,704
Okay, so so this idea of using model-based uh planning is really old.

333
00:42:14,864 --> 00:42:19,584
It goes back to the late 50s in the USSR and early 60s in the West.

334
00:42:20,864 --> 00:42:26,948
and the main issue we're going to have to face is how do we prevent those systems from collapsing when we train them?

335
00:42:28,464 --> 00:42:33,984
and one trick is you use an objective function that has two terms.

336
00:42:34,144 --> 00:42:36,864
One term is minimize the prediction error.

337
00:42:36,944 --> 00:42:42,820
So try to predict SY as well as you can from SX and perhaps the action if you have an action.

338
00:42:43,566 --> 00:42:51,332
But at the same time, try to make the representation extracted by the encoder as informative as possible about the input.

339
00:42:52,206 --> 00:42:52,464
Okay.

340
00:42:53,184 --> 00:43:01,344
So I represent this by this information content i of SX and is a minus sign you found because you want to minimize uh minimize it.

341
00:43:01,504 --> 00:43:03,300
So maximize the information content.

342
00:43:03,566 --> 00:43:05,024
Now here is the problem though.

343
00:43:05,184 --> 00:43:08,292
We don't have any good ways of measuring information content.

344
00:43:09,614 --> 00:43:24,676
We have bad estimates of information content that are all upper bounds, and that's terrible because we want to maximize information content, and maximizing an upper bound kind of is not a well-principled way of maximizing a quantity.

345
00:43:25,584 --> 00:43:26,784
but that's what we have.

346
00:43:26,944 --> 00:43:29,572
So that's what we're going to do.

347
00:43:29,904 --> 00:43:44,548
so over the last five years or so, we've come up with a bunch of different methods to uh to to sort of you know estimate the information content in a differentiable way so we can maximize it as the cost function of some neural net.

348
00:43:47,822 --> 00:43:49,424
oh man, I didn't update this slide.

349
00:43:49,664 --> 00:43:58,308
a search for for Jeppa, John Tabini predictive architecture between double codes on Google Scholar returns not 1700 papers, 3700.

350
00:44:00,302 --> 00:44:02,116
That slide is six months old.

351
00:44:02,384 --> 00:44:05,664
It's becoming a very popular topic.

352
00:44:05,934 --> 00:44:08,036
Okay, so how are we going to measure information?

353
00:44:08,544 --> 00:44:12,324
let's say we run a batch of samples through our encoders.

354
00:44:12,910 --> 00:44:18,628
What we get on the output is sort of a matrix for every sample that we run, we get a representation vector.

355
00:44:19,374 --> 00:44:22,304
Okay, and for E, and so we get a matrix, right?

356
00:44:22,384 --> 00:44:24,132
We run a bunch of samples through it.

357
00:44:25,104 --> 00:44:28,196
we want that matrix to be maximally informative.

358
00:44:29,744 --> 00:44:33,636
that matrix cannot invent information, it's a deterministic function of the input.

359
00:44:34,224 --> 00:44:36,704
so how do we make it informative?

360
00:44:36,864 --> 00:44:40,548
First of all, we probably want the rows to be different from each other.

361
00:44:41,070 --> 00:44:45,060
If the system produces the same representation for every sample, not very informative.

362
00:44:45,582 --> 00:44:57,744
We probably also want the columns to be all different from each other, because if they're all the same, that means the system basically is cheating, it's not carrying a lot of information, it's producing the same variable multiple times.

363
00:44:58,670 --> 00:45:02,544
and we've come up with a number of different criteria to kind of do this.

364
00:45:02,766 --> 00:45:08,464
So some of them are simple contrastive, they try to repel the rows in that matrix.

365
00:45:08,782 --> 00:45:10,704
Those are called contrastive methods.

366
00:45:10,864 --> 00:45:21,104
And there's another set that try to repel the columns in that matrix, but as it turns out, the best methods are neither, they are a little different from that.

367
00:45:21,184 --> 00:45:25,348
So there's one in particular that I like called SIGREG, which I'm going to tell you a little bit about.

368
00:45:27,534 --> 00:45:33,060
so SIGREG, it belongs to a family of other methods that are based on clustering.

369
00:45:33,264 --> 00:45:49,988
So SIGREG essentially takes each of those samples and projects those vectors, representation vectors, and it projects those vectors against many different dimensions, which are random actually, and they change every time.

370
00:45:50,926 --> 00:45:55,972
Okay, so take your representation vector, project it against many random directions.

371
00:45:57,518 --> 00:46:02,916
Now, over the batch, each of those projections is going to be a univariate distribution.

372
00:46:03,854 --> 00:46:04,080
Okay?

373
00:46:04,782 --> 00:46:13,700
And what Creg is based on is try to make that empirical marginal distribution as close to a Gaussian as possible.

374
00:46:14,960 --> 00:46:33,732
So you take those samples that you see here, those red dots, you project the red dots against several dimensions, you get a marginal distribution, which is empirical, and you try to make this empirical distribution as close as possible to a Gaussian.

375
00:46:36,078 --> 00:46:38,000
there are several ways to do this.

376
00:46:39,278 --> 00:46:46,800
we use a technique based on Fourier transform, but you can use the cumulative distribution, distance between cumulative distribution, and that works pretty well.

377
00:46:48,654 --> 00:47:04,644
But there is this fabulous theorem that says if all the marginals of a joint distribution against all dimensions are all Gaussian with variance one, the joint distribution is Gaussian, isotropic.

378
00:47:05,070 --> 00:47:05,280
Okay?

379
00:47:06,030 --> 00:47:16,964
So C means sketch isotropic Gaussian regularization because it drives the overall distribution on the output of the encoder to be a isotropic Gaussian with variance one.

380
00:47:17,440 --> 00:47:22,160
And it works really well, and there is world models that are built on top of this.

381
00:47:22,240 --> 00:47:24,132
So this is the world model.

382
00:47:24,560 --> 00:47:26,400
I didn't pick the name, okay.

383
00:47:26,480 --> 00:47:27,840
I have to apologize for that.

384
00:47:30,638 --> 00:47:35,172
This there's a whole series of models that are called le something, and I have nothing to do with it, okay.

385
00:47:36,206 --> 00:47:37,600
I couldn't talk them out of it.

386
00:47:39,630 --> 00:47:52,400
and this is a world model trained using this uh SIGREG technique, and it you know it can be applied to like simple toy problems of planning, like the push-t problem, or or you know, simple kind of robot control.

387
00:47:52,480 --> 00:47:53,920
I'm not going to bore you with details.

388
00:47:54,080 --> 00:47:56,080
There's a theorem, which is pretty cool.

389
00:47:56,160 --> 00:48:05,860
that says: if your data actually is an isotropic Gaussian, or not isotropic, but is a joint Gaussian on the input.

